WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.310
Today the greatest risk of global catastrophe
doesn’t look like this.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:09.260
Instead  it looks like this.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:12.700
One of the most frustrating aspects of the
novel coronavirus pandemic

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:18.420
is how many early warning signs and response
plans for just such an event were waved away

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:21.420
by public health officials, the president,
governors,

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:24.530
and just about everyone else who could have
made a difference.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:29.560
The George W. Bush administration nearly put
in place a pandemic preparedness plan...

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:35.660
I’m here to discuss our strategy to protect
and prevent the American people

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.980
from a possible outbreak.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.240
And then let it drop.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.500
What a problem, came out of nowhere.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:47.700
It’s important to underscore that all the
death and economic wreckage COVID-19 has caused

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.700
didn’t just “come out of nowhere”

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:53.460
because there’s another totally predictable
crisis

00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:57.590
that promises to be even more damaging to
our way of life.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.350
The national debt—the amount of money that
the federal government owes—

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.269
is already choking down economic growth,

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:07.160
but in the future it could lead to a “sudden
inflation,”

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:11.480
and “a loss of confidence in the federal
government’s ability or commitment

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.520
to repay  its debts in full,”

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.990
and “such a crisis could spread globally.”

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:19.450
That’s according to the nonpartisan Congressional
Budget Office,

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:25.780
which has been warning Americans for years about the
long-term consequence of the ballooning debt.

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:31.640
Like the coronavirus, the debt problem has
the potential to seemingly appear out of the blue

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.800
and turn our world upside down in a matter
of weeks.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.880
There’s plenty we can do to avoid or minimize
the potential shock to our system,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.020
but congress and presidents from both major
parties

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.260
have accepted Dick Cheney’s false maxim
that “deficits don’t matter.”

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.380
Instead, they just keep spending more than
we take in even during the good times,

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:59.020
and being so deeply in hock will make us less
able to deal with a future crisis.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.920
The amount of money the government owed to
the public was 79 percent of Gross Domestic Product

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.820
at the end of 2019, up from 31 percent in
2001.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.920
The COVID-19 Lockdown and emergency spending

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:17.220
will push the curve above 100 percent of
GDP by the end of 2020

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.380
and it’s expected to keep rising.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:24.820
Emergency spending and plunging tax revenues
are making a bad situation worse.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:28.440
The Congressional Budget Office forecasts
that the budget deficit this year

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.060
will be  17.9 percent of GDP,

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.959
meaning that the government is running much
larger deficits

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:38.080
than it did even at the height of the financial
crisis.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.280
Economists, such as Nobel prize-winner Paul
Krugman and proponents of modern monetary theory

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.200
look at the absence of inflation and higher
interest rates so far

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.880
as justification for ever-more spending and
borrowing.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.220
While it’s true that the cost of paying
interest on the debt

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:55.880
is still dwarfed by other expenditures,

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.300
that’s because historically low interest
rates have made government borrowing cheap.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:04.020
But there’s no reason to believe that interest
rates won’t rise over time.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:07.540
According to conservative estimates from the
Congressional Budget Office,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.620
as the total budget grows as a percentage
of GDP,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:14.000
the cost of paying interest on the  debt will increase faster,

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.960
until it accounts for about 24 cents of every
dollar spent by 2050.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:23.720
And these estimates don’t take into account
emergency spending for COVID-19,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:27.190
which will make servicing the debt even more
costly over time.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:30.440
Like a monthly credit card payment that eats
into a household budget,

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.489
federal debt means less money to buy other
things.

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:37.410
And when governments run large, persistent
deficits,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:41.860
it also has a devastating impact on economic
growth over time.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:47.680
Our current debt levels could reduce Gross
Domestic Product by about one quarter over 23 years,

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:52.500
according to research by Harvard economists
Carmen Reinhart and Kenneth Rogoff.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:57.420
It’s a case of what French economist Frédéric
Bastiat referred to as “the unseen”

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:01.709
because we’ll never get to experience how
much wealthier we otherwise would have been

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:05.099
had the federal government practiced fiscal
prudence.

00:04:05.099 --> 00:04:09.220
Anemic growth will impact the poorest American’s
most of all,

00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:12.680
causing their material progress to slow considerably.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:18.120
It means less leisure time, smaller homes,
older cars, and less health care.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:19.720
In the short-term, there’s no question

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.720
that the government can and will be able to borrow massively,

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.060
and interest rates are likely to stay low
for the time being as the world shifts into recession.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:31.600
But there’s also the specter of investors
here and abroad

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.719
refusing to buy US-issued debt as our economy
flattens,

00:04:35.719 --> 00:04:38.729
China flexes its economic and political might,

00:04:38.729 --> 00:04:43.120
and alternative instruments such as bitcoin
and gold offer safe refuge.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:48.600
Like a global pandemic, a debt crisis seems
impossibly off in the distance

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.700
until it’s the only thing you can see.

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:56.080
Even the most hubristic economist or president
would have to admit

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:01.360
that there will come a time when the U.S.
dollar is no longer the world’s reserve currency.

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.780
That change won’t necessarily be as dramatic

00:05:03.789 --> 00:05:08.189
as when German paper marks became worthless
after World War I,

00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:14.080
but it will massively reduce purchasing power
even as it increases the cost of everything.

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:19.120
Spending proponents sometimes cite World War
II when talking about battling COVID-19

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:23.020
and the analogy is apt for reasons they may
not intend.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:29.320
Between 1940 and 1945, federal spending increased
tenfold from $10 billion

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.860
to over $100 billion to pay for the war effort.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:37.820
But when victory was won, the government immediately
cut military spending.

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:42.440
Once peace-time growth resumed, the debt-to-GDP
ratio fell quickly.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:48.710
But starting in the 1970s, with the exception
of a 5-year dip during the economic boom of the 1990s,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:53.260
the federal government has been growing the
size of the debt in relation to GDP.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:54.720
Instead of a foreign enemy,

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:59.479
our out-of-control spending has been driven
by the persistent rise in the cost of entitlements

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:01.520
such as Medicare and Social Security.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.860
Even as total tax revenues increase, we keep
spending more and more,

00:06:05.860 --> 00:06:11.260
adding to a national debt that costs more
to service even as it reduces economic growth.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.720
Despite seemingly just showing up in America
without notice,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.960
the coronavirus pandemic and all that has
happened over the past two months

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:20.930
didn’t “just come out of nowhere.”

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:25.080
It was there all along, barely lurking in
the shadows.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:29.400
When the debt crisis materializes and our
options are severely limited

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:32.339
because of decades of profligate spending,

00:06:32.339 --> 00:06:34.999
politicians sitting in the Oval Office and
Congress

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:40.900
will claim that it all just came out of nowhere,
like that crazy virus back in 2020.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:46.020
But nothing could be farther from the truth:
Budget wonks are sounding the alarm.

00:06:46.029 --> 00:06:48.330
We need to heed these warnings now

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:52.740
or suffer an economic lockdown later from
which there may be no escape.

